909672581228832,"slope":0.00033157513115721765,"total":0.0},"snow_normal":0.0},"titles":{"year":"2026 so far: 2 daily record highs, 1 daily record low","records":"Albany has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Albany's days and nights have warmed since 1982","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 64\u00b0F was the 25th coolest November 13 high (tied) of 126 since 1892, the coolest since 2022.","t_yest":"November 13: cooler than 80% of years","p_yest":"The high of <strong>64\u00b0F</strong> was the 25th coolest November 13 high (tied) of 126 since 1892, the coolest since 2022. The normal high for the date is 71\u00b0F, and the record is 90\u00b0F (1915). The low of <strong>39\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Albany has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Albany's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Albany has set <strong class=\"sig\">16 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 78 that chance alone would give, and <strong>86 record highs</strong>, close to the 78 that chance alone would give. The 1910s hold the most record highs of any decade (41). Albany's all-time high is 107\u00b0F, on July 15, 1980.","t_chg":"Albany's days and nights have warmed since 1982","p_chg":"Across 113 complete years: Albany's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.12\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.24 to +0.00) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Albany's average high shows no clear change: +0.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (0.00 to +0.13) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1982, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.61\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +1.27 to +1.95) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.60\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.30 to +0.91). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; January and April fell. Highs rose in 3 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Albany's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.25 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 46.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 1964 (71.46 in); the driest, 2011 (26.79 in). Albany almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 2 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}